description:
Define the core typed loaded-item contracts and collapse `resourceKind` into the new app-facing `type`.

## Background
The app currently has overlapping concepts (`BaseResource`, `resourceKind`, `Project`, `LoadedBaseResource`) that blur disk representation, loader output, and UI-facing shape. The new source of truth for app-facing branching should be one discriminated union by `type`, with initial variants `usfmScripture` and `translationNotes`. `containerFormat` remains low-level container/storage metadata only.

This bead is the architectural keystone for the rest of the refactor. It explicitly supersedes the older March 23 `BaseResource / Project` model described in `product-docs/specs/base-resource-project-architecture.md`. Implementers should not preserve that older two-layer worldview in parallel. This bead defines the new source of truth.

## Type Definitions to Emit

The bead must produce these types (or equivalent after naming review):

```ts
// App-facing discriminant - the ONE primary classification seam
type LibraryItemType = "usfmScripture" | "translationNotes";

// Low-level storage metadata only - NOT a UI/service branching seam
type ContainerFormat = "resource-container" | "scripture-burrito";

// Base shape shared by all typed items
type LibraryItemBase = {
  id: string;
  displayName: string;
  managedPath: string;
  containerFormat: ContainerFormat;
  language: {
    code: string;
    name: string;
    direction: "ltr" | "rtl";
  };
  capabilities: LibraryItemCapabilities;
};

// Scripture item - type-specific verbs live HERE, not on the library seam
type UsfmScriptureItem = LibraryItemBase & {
  type: "usfmScripture";
  readWorkspace(): Promise<ScriptureWorkspaceSnapshot>;
  readBook(bookCode: string): Promise<ScriptureBook | null>;
  saveBook?(bookCode: string, contents: string): Promise<void>;
  addBook?(bookCode: string): Promise<void>;
};

// TN item - returns raw markdown at verse layer; rendering stays in UI
type TranslationNotesItem = LibraryItemBase & {
  type: "translationNotes";
  listBookCodes(): Promise<readonly string[]>;
  readBook(bookCode: string): Promise<PackedTranslationNotesBook | null>;
  readChapter(
    bookCode: string,
    chapterNumber: number,
  ): Promise<Record<string, string> | null>; // verse → raw markdown
};

// The discriminated union - callers switch on .type once only
type LibraryItem = UsfmScriptureItem | TranslationNotesItem;
```

## Technical Approach
- Define the new app-facing typed item union in core persistence/domain code and make the naming decision explicit.
- Preserve any low-level metadata needed for import/index/load flows, but move app-facing branching away from `resourceKind`.
- Keep the current scripture `Project` behavior operational by expressing it as the `usfmScripture` typed variant rather than a separate top-level worldview.
- Decide, document, and encode where `type` lives versus where `containerFormat` and future capability attachment live.
- Audit the current public seams so there is no ambiguity about whether consumers should branch on `type`, `containerFormat`, or capability.

## Repo Touchpoints
- `src/core/persistence/BaseResource.ts`
- `src/core/persistence/LoadedBaseResource.ts`
- `src/core/persistence/BaseResourceCapabilities.ts`
- `tests/unit/core/persistence/baseResourceContracts.test.ts`
- `tests/unit/core/persistence/baseResourceCapabilities.test.ts`
- `product-docs/specs/base-resource-project-architecture.md`

## Success Criteria
- There is exactly one primary app-facing classification axis: `type`.
- `containerFormat` remains available only as storage/container metadata and not as the UI/service branching seam.
- The typed contract is clear enough that downstream loader, import, index, service, and UI beads can reference it without re-litigating terminology.
- The repo no longer treats the March 23 `BaseResource / Project` ADR as the governing architecture for this area.

## Acceptance Criteria
- Core typed item union exists and is the single app-facing classification seam.
- `resourceKind` no longer drives app/service/UI branching.
- `containerFormat` is retained only as low-level metadata.
- This bead explicitly supersedes, rather than amends, the older `BaseResource / Project` ADR.

## Test Plan
- Unit tests for typed contract helpers, narrowing behavior, and capability composition.
- Regression tests for any current app-boundary code that relied on `resourceKind`.
- Compile-time coverage showing invalid combinations are rejected.

## Considerations
- Downstream beads should assume the typed-item model is the new architecture and should migrate callers away from the older `BaseResource / Project` framing rather than trying to keep both first-class.

Define the capability model so capabilities are behavioral add-ons, not replacements for typed app-facing contracts.

## Background
Capabilities are still the right mechanism for affordances such as editability, remote sync, and anchor navigation, but they should not flatten very different loaded shapes into one fake generic content model. The typed loaded item determines the data contract. Capabilities answer what the app may do with that item.

## Capability Model to Emit

```ts
type SupportedEditor = "usfmScripture";

type LibraryItemCapabilities = {
  editableWith?: SupportedEditor;
  remoteSync?: RemoteSyncCapability;
  anchorNavigation?: AnchorNavigationCapability;
};

interface RemoteSyncCapability {
  kind: "remoteSync";
  source: {
    kind: "git" | "url" | "unknown";
    identifier: string;
    ref?: string;
  };
  applyUpdate(): Promise<void>;
}

interface AnchorNavigationCapability {
  kind: "anchorNavigation";
}
```

Rule:
- `type` determines the app-facing contract (defined in `0fx`).
- `capabilities` determine what the app may do with that item.
- There should be one source of truth in code for what is editable by type.
- Top-level library APIs stay generic; type-specific verbs live on the typed item nouns.

## Technical Approach
- Keep capabilities interface-shaped where behavior matters rather than collapsing them into booleans.
- Define the first stable capabilities needed by the new model: editable behavior, remote sync/update affordances, and scripture-anchor navigation.
- Make it explicit which current scripture behaviors remain type-specific API on `usfmScripture` and which become reusable capabilities.
- Avoid inventing a second discriminator that competes with `type`.

## Repo Touchpoints
- `src/core/persistence/BaseResourceCapabilities.ts`
- `src/core/persistence/BaseResource.ts`
- `tests/unit/core/persistence/baseResourceCapabilities.test.ts`

## Success Criteria
- Capability definitions are explicit, composable, and non-overlapping with the `type` discriminant.
- The model can represent future combinations such as editable non-scripture content without requiring a new top-level noun.
- Downstream beads can answer "branch on type or capability?" deterministically.

## Acceptance Criteria
- Capability interfaces are defined without competing with `type`.
- Current scripture and TN flows can be modeled without a second top-level noun.
- Downstream implementers can tell when to branch on capability versus type.

## Test Plan
- Unit tests for capability guards/helpers.
- Type-level/compile-time tests for typed-item plus capability composition.
- Regression coverage for current scripture and TN assumptions.

## Considerations
- Downstream beads should attach capabilities at load time, not compute them ad hoc at render time.
- Capability attachment is the loader's job; UI just reads what the loader attached.

Replace the broad `ProjectsService` worldview with a `LibraryService`-style top-level seam and focused internal subservices.

## Background
The current top-level app service already orchestrates much more than scripture projects, but its name and shape still reflect the older scripture-first worldview. The clarified model needs one library-oriented seam that can list typed items, open typed items, import them, and manage the index while delegating specialized behavior internally.

This bead should be implemented against the new typed-item architecture that supersedes the March 23 `BaseResource / Project` ADR. The service split should not try to preserve the older two-layer model as the main governing shape. It should also explicitly resolve the current `DefaultProjectsService` naming confusion: this is no longer "the scripture one," it is the broader library/app orchestration seam.

## LibraryService Interface to Produce

```ts
interface LibraryService {
  // Listing - returns indexed facts, not full loaded items
  listLibraryItems(): Promise<IndexedLibraryItem[]>;
  listCurrentProjects(): Promise<IndexedLibraryItem[]>; // isEditable subset

  // Opening - returns typed runtime contracts, not generic readers
  openItem(itemRef: string): Promise<LibraryItem | null>;

  // Import
  importFolder(source: string, options?: ImportOptions): Promise<ImportResult>;
  importZip(source: string, options?: ImportOptions): Promise<ImportResult>;
  importRemoteZip(source: ImportSource, options?: ImportOptions): Promise<ImportResult>;

  // Index management
  reconcileIndex(): Promise<void>;
}

// Indexed catalog row - fast listing facts only, not content
type IndexedLibraryItem = {
  managedPath: string;
  displayName: string;
  type: LibraryItemType;        // "usfmScripture" | "translationNotes"
  containerFormat: ContainerFormat; // "resource-container" | "scripture-burrito"
  isEditable: boolean;
  hasRemoteSync: boolean;
  languageCode: string;
  languageName: string;
};
```

Rule: The top-level library seam is generic. Type-specific verbs live on the typed `LibraryItem` nouns returned by `openItem()`.

## Technical Approach
- Introduce an explicit library-facing service contract and naming surface.
- Split internal concerns into focused helpers for import, typed item opening, editable scripture operations, and read-only/reference operations.
- Keep the app-facing surface concise: list items, open typed item, open current editable scripture item, import, delete, rename, reconcile.
- Preserve behavior during migration through adapters if necessary, but make the target seam unmistakable.
- Resolve the `DefaultProjectsService` role question directly: it becomes `DefaultLibraryService`.

## Repo Touchpoints
- `src/core/persistence/ProjectsService.ts`
- `src/app/persistence/DefaultProjectsService.ts`
- `src/app/services/LibraryService.ts` (new)

## Success Criteria
- The app-facing orchestration seam is library-oriented, not scripture-first.
- `openItem()` returns typed `LibraryItem` variants, not a generic reader.
- Editable/current-project behavior derives from `capabilities` on the indexed items.
- `ProjectsService` is an adapter alias or removed.

## Acceptance Criteria
- One top-level seam that handles listing, opening, importing, and index reconciliation.
- No scripture-specific `openProject()` method on the top-level library interface.
- Remote TN orchestration sits under the clarified library seam.

## Test Plan
- Unit tests for library listing and editable filtering.
- Service/integration tests for open item / open editable scripture item flows.
- Regression tests proving service terminology no longer implies the older ADR.

## Considerations
- Adapter shims may be needed during migration but should not remain as permanent architecture.
